#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
tc: file mailbox between agent sessions (Claude Code, Codex) on one machine.

One session (the one you talk to) relays for others you can't reach directly.
All run as the same Linux user, or as users in one group once `tc init
--shared` has run, so a shared directory is the whole transport.
One message is one markdown file, so a human can read the mailbox with cat.

Layout under ROOT (default: this script's directory, override with TC_ROOT):
  mail/to-<name>/NNNNNN-from-<sender>.md   unread
  mail/to-<name>/read/...                  read (moved there by `tc read`)
  status/<name>.md                         latest self-reported status
  mail/.seq                                message counter (flock guarded)
  mail/.watch-<name>.lock                  held by `tc watch <name>` while it runs
  mail/.box-<name>.lock                    the inbox lock: send, read, card, codex join (D14)
  mail/.ring-pending-<name>                ring owed and why, or clear (D14, D16, D20)
  cards/<name>.json                        one address card per name (C1)
  .shared                                  the group a shared mailbox is shared with (C4), 0644 (D21)

@interacts  writes mail/, status/ and cards/ under ROOT; no network. The waking side is
            Claude Code's background Bash: `tc wait` or `tc watch` exiting is
            what re-invokes the session that started it. `health` and `watch`
            also read /proc (meminfo, locks, each process's comm and cmdline)
            and run `nvidia-smi` when it exists. They never signal or touch
            another process. `send` to a codex card runs `codex queue` (C2)
            in a session of its own, and kills that one process group if the
            ring times out. While ROOT/.shared exists (C4) every command
            runs under umask 002 and every folder tc makes gets the group
            it names and mode 2775 (make_folder, D16), or 3775 (sticky,
            D17) for mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ themselves, or
            3770 for an inbox and its read/, which only `join` makes, as
            the recipient (D20). Messages, cards and status files are made
            0640, never group-writable; mail/.seq, the locks and the ring
            marker stay 0664 and are never removed (the marker is
            rewritten, D20). A send to a name with no card, whether or not
            an inbox is there (D21), or to an inbox its card's owner does
            not own, is refused, exit 1 (D20). `init` reads the group
            database, makes the four when missing (D19), sets the group
            and mode of every folder and file under them that is not right
            yet, then writes .shared at 0644, owner-write only (D21),
            unless it already names the group; then its owner's rerun
            sets it to 0644. When .shared names another group and
            another user owns it, init refuses before it changes
            anything (D21). Shared
            or not, the locks, mail/.seq, status files and the ring marker
            are opened without following a symlink, and an inbox or its
            read/ is lstat()ed before use: a link at any of them is
            refused, exit 1 (D17). A FIFO or anything else that is not a
            regular file at a lock, mail/.seq or a status file is refused
            at once, exit 1, never waited on (D19). Messages are read only
            from regular files: read, peek and log name anything else and
            never read it, read leaves it in place, and wait, the counts
            and health leave it out; health and watch never take a linked
            lock for a listener's (D19).
@deps       python3 stdlib (argparse, collections, contextlib, errno, fcntl,
            grp, json, pathlib, pwd, re, resource, shlex, shutil, signal,
            stat, subprocess, time, datetime, os, sys); Linux only (flock,
            RLIMIT_AS, O_NOFOLLOW, O_NONBLOCK, /proc). nvidia-smi optional; the codex CLI for the codex route.
@complexity send O(b), b body bytes up to SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES; status O(1).
            read/peek/wait-poll O(u), u unread messages in one inbox,
            single digits in practice. log O(t), t total messages ever,
            low hundreds per job. health O(p + k + n + m + c): p processes
            on the machine (hundreds), k lines in /proc/locks (tens), n
            mailbox names and c cards (a few dozen each), m message files in
            every inbox and read/ (one stat each: hundreds to thousands, and
            it grows with the history). Each watch poll O(k + n) plus one
            nvidia-smi run where it exists; it reads no cards. O(p) once per
            heartbeat. join O(1), or O(u) for a codex card's unread count;
            card O(u) for its unread count; cards O(c). init O(f) over f
            folders and files under the shared folders (hundreds to
            thousands; read/ is the history). .shared is read once in main
            (set_shared_umask), then once per step that depends on it
            (mailbox_share, and each make_folder call, once per call):
            log and cards 1 in all; peek, wait (never per poll), status,
            watch, health and init 2; read and card 3; send 4; join up to
            5 (a codex join that makes the inbox). A private send, read,
            peek or wait that makes an inbox's read/ adds 1. Each read is
            one small file, O(1), and each make_folder call one group
            lookup when shared.
@alloc      one file per message, kept forever (read/ is the history); one
            card per name, replaced on a rejoin; one inbox lock file per
            name (mail/.box-<name>.lock), kept; one ring marker per codex
            name (mail/.ring-pending-<name>), kept: rewritten, never
            removed (D20); ROOT/.shared, written by init and kept.
            Nothing held in memory past one command. `wait` measured flat at
            13 MB RSS over 200k polls; it also hard-caps itself at
            WAIT_MEMORY_CAP_BYTES so a runaway dies alone. `wait` holds
            mail/.wait-<name>.lock for its lifetime; the kernel releases it
            when the process exits or is killed.
"""

import argparse
import collections
import contextlib
import datetime
import errno
import fcntl
import grp
import json
import os
import pathlib
import pwd
import re
import resource
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time

ROOT = pathlib.Path(os.environ.get("TC_ROOT", pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
MAIL = ROOT / "mail"
STATUS = ROOT / "status"
SEQ_FILE = MAIL / ".seq"
SEQ_WIDTH = 6
POLL_SECONDS = 5
# Claude Code caps a background Bash at 7200 s. Exit before that so the
# session gets a clean "timeout, re-arm" instead of a killed command.
DEFAULT_WAIT_SECONDS = 6600
EXIT_TIMEOUT = 2
EXIT_ALREADY_LISTENING = 3
# Address-space ceiling for a listener. Measured need is about 13 MB; the cap
# only exists so a bug can never take the machine down with it.
WAIT_MEMORY_CAP_BYTES = 256 * 1024 * 1024
EXIT_ALERT = 5
# CARDS R6 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): a usage error (D12). argparse's own
# code is 2, the same as a wait timeout, so a mistyped listener would read as
# a timeout and re-arm the same typo in a loop of paid wakes.
EXIT_USAGE = 9
# CARDS R1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): mailbox names, contract C0.
# A name becomes a folder name here and a word on a remote command line over
# ssh (C3). So: letters, digits, dot, dash, underscore, at most this many,
# never a leading dash (it would read as an option), never only dots (a
# folder that means "here" or "up").
NAME_MAX_CHARS = 64
NAME_PATTERN = re.compile(rf"[A-Za-z0-9._][A-Za-z0-9._-]{{0,{NAME_MAX_CHARS - 1}}}")
# Row 6 of the exit table (contracts.md), each refused before anything is
# written: a bad name under C0, for any name argument including
# --reports-to; a name that reports to itself (join, card); a bad ssh target
# or a bad remote tc path (card).
EXIT_BAD_NAME = 6
# CARDS R2 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): address cards, contract C1.
# Row 1 of the exit table, refused with words. send: an empty message, an
# unreadable card, a card with no route, a route not built yet, a message
# .tmp that cannot be made. join: --kind codex with no thread, a
# CODEX_THREAD_ID that is not a thread id. join and card: a card .tmp that
# cannot be made. card: unread mail here without --force. Today's
# empty-message exit already meant this.
EXIT_REFUSED = 1
# One JSON file per name. A path built from ROOT, like MAIL and STATUS.
CARDS = ROOT / "cards"
# The card's own format number, so a later shape can be told apart.
CARD_VERSION = 1
# Phase fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the most of one card file
# tc reads. A card is eleven short fields, a few hundred bytes; 64 KiB is
# far past any card tc writes, and it bounds what one hand-made file can
# make tc health, tc cards or a send read. A file over it is unreadable.
CARD_READ_MAX_BYTES = 65536
# C1's kinds of session, and the only values `tc join --kind` takes. A codex
# card is the one the codex route rings; claude and other route local.
CARD_KINDS = ("claude", "codex", "other")
# The account word when nothing names one. C1: never a blank.
UNKNOWN_ACCOUNT = "unknown"
# Where tc lives on the other machine when `tc card` is not told (C1).
DEFAULT_REMOTE_TC = "~/TheConnection/tc"
# C1: user@host or host alone, no leading dash on either part, no spaces.
# The target is a word on ssh's command line, where a dash means an option.
SSH_TARGET_PATTERN = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._][A-Za-z0-9._-]*(@[A-Za-z0-9._][A-Za-z0-9._-]*)?")
# C1: a remote tc path starts with one of these.
REMOTE_TC_PREFIXES = ("/", "~/")
# CARDS R3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the router (C2) and the size limit (C0).
# This is a mailbox for words: 1 MiB is far past any message a session
# writes, and small enough that a runaway pipe never fills the disk.
SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES = 1048576
SEND_TOO_LARGE_WORDS = "tc send: message over 1 MiB; this is a mailbox for words"
# A message over SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES, refused before it takes a number.
EXIT_TOO_LARGE = 8
# CARDS R4 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the codex route (C2, D3).
# How long `codex queue` gets to answer. It only queues a line for the
# thread, so a healthy answer is quick; past this the sender stops waiting.
# The mail has landed before the ring either way.
CODEX_RING_SECONDS = 30
# CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D14 and the audit's fixes.
# The shape of a Codex thread id: letters, digits, dot, dash, underscore, a
# letter or digit first. The value goes into codex's argv right after
# --thread, where a leading dash could read as a flag, so nothing of another
# shape (or type) ever gets there (D14).
THREAD_ID_PATTERN = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*")
# How a .tmp file is made: create it, fail if anything at all already has
# that name (O_EXCL), and never follow a link (O_NOFOLLOW). A link planted at
# a .tmp name someone can guess is refused, never written through.
NEW_FILE_FLAGS = os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW
# The new file's mode before the umask, the same as open() gives one.
NEW_FILE_MODE = 0o666
# Phase fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D16, the ring marker's
# states and the backoff after a ring that timed out.
# The first line of mail/.ring-pending-<name> says why a ring is owed, or
# that none is (RING_CLEAR, below). Only RING_TIMED_OUT holds later rings back. codex's own words go on the second
# line, so nothing codex prints can pass for a state.
RING_JOINED = "joined with unread mail"
RING_RINGING = "ringing"
RING_FAILED = "failed"
RING_TIMED_OUT = "timed out"
# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20 item 5. The marker
# is never removed: a ring that exits 0, or a codex join with nothing
# waiting, rewrites its first line to this, and nothing is owed. The other
# words above are its pending states, and RING_TIMED_OUT its backoff.
RING_CLEAR = "clear"
# After a ring times out, sends to that name skip the ring for this long. A
# hung `codex queue` would otherwise cost every send its full
# CODEX_RING_SECONDS (ten sends, 300 s of the senders' time). Five minutes
# rides out a short codex outage, and the marker stays, so the first send
# after it rings again. A ring that fails fast (codex exits non-zero) costs
# one process start, so it holds nothing back.
RING_BACKOFF_SECONDS = 300
# How the backoff's words name a time: the local clock to the second, which
# is all a sender needs to know when a send rings again.
CLOCK_FORMAT = "%H:%M:%S"
# The most of a ring marker tc reads: two short lines, so 4 KiB bounds a
# hand-made one; only its first line is used.
RING_MARKER_READ_MAX_BYTES = 4096
# SHARED H1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the shared mailbox, contract C4 and D16.
# The file under ROOT whose presence makes the mailbox shared. It holds the
# group's name on one line, written by `tc init --shared --group <g>`.
SHARED_MARKER_NAME = ".shared"
# The most of .shared tc reads. A group name is a few dozen characters at
# most, so 4 KiB bounds a hand-made file without ever cutting a real one.
SHARED_MARKER_READ_MAX_BYTES = 4096
# SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): .shared's mode. The user who
# ran init reads and writes it; every other member only reads it, so no
# other member can change which group everyone's tc uses. Init's umask
# (SHARED_UMASK) takes no bit from it, so a rewrite on a rerun is 0644 too.
SHARED_MARKER_MODE = 0o644
# The folders a shared mailbox shares, by name under ROOT (C4). outbox/ is
# the ssh route's (C3); init reaches it whenever it is there.
SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES = ("mail", "status", "cards", "outbox")
# The umask while .shared exists: the locks, mail/.seq and the ring
# marker tc makes are group-writable (0664), so every member can take a
# lock, a number or ring. Message, card and status files pass
# SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE to os.open instead, and the umask cannot add bits.
SHARED_UMASK = 0o002
# A shared folder: owner and group read, write and enter it, and setgid,
# so whatever is made inside takes the folder's group, not the maker's.
SHARED_FOLDER_MODE = 0o2775
# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20, nobody touches
# another member's mail.
# The files every member must write, directly in mail/: mail/.seq, the
# locks and the ring marker. Owner and group read and write them. The
# worst a member can do through one is make a send fail or ring once more.
SHARED_WRITABLE_FILE_MODE = 0o664
# Message, card and status files (D20 item 2): the owner reads and writes,
# the group only reads, so no member rewrites another's file in place.
SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE = 0o640
# An inbox and its read/ (D20 item 1), owned by the recipient: setgid,
# sticky, group read, write and enter, nothing for other users. Under
# sticky only a file's owner or the folder's can delete or rename it, so
# senders cannot touch each other's mail, and the recipient's tc read
# still moves all of it into read/.
SHARED_INBOX_MODE = 0o3770
# SHARED D17 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): no planted links.
# mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ themselves: SHARED_FOLDER_MODE plus the
# sticky bit, so in them only an entry's owner (or the folder's) may delete
# or rename it, as in /tmp: no member removes or swaps another user's inbox,
# card, lock or status. An inbox and its read/ take SHARED_INBOX_MODE.
SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE = 0o3775
# The most of one status file `tc status` shows. A status is one argument
# word, which Linux caps at 128 KiB; 1 MiB is far past that and bounds what
# a hand-made file can make it print.
STATUS_READ_MAX_BYTES = 1048576
# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D19, messages are read
# only from regular files. A message's header line and the blank line after
# it run to a few hundred bytes (a number, two names of at most
# NAME_MAX_CHARS, a time); 4 KiB leaves room for the gate's note (C3).
MESSAGE_HEADER_MAX_BYTES = 4096
# The most of one message file that read, peek and log read: the largest
# body a send writes, plus its header. A file over it is named, not shown.
MESSAGE_READ_MAX_BYTES = SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES + MESSAGE_HEADER_MAX_BYTES

# Prompt cache. A Claude session's cache expires after this long with no
# request. Waking a session sooner than that keeps its cache warm. Override
# with TC_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS if your plan uses a different TTL.
CACHE_TTL_SECONDS = int(os.environ.get("TC_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS", 3600))
# Wake this long before the TTL runs out, to cover the poll interval and the
# time the session takes to respond. The margin shrinks with short TTLs (a
# twelfth of the TTL at most) so a 5-minute TTL still waits 275 s, not 0.
KEEPALIVE_MARGIN_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("TC_KEEPALIVE_MARGIN_SECONDS", 300))
KEEPALIVE_MARGIN_MAX_FRACTION = 1 / 12
MIN_KEEPALIVE_SECONDS = 1

# `tc watch` defaults. A breach wakes the session early; otherwise it wakes
# once per heartbeat with a one-line summary.
WATCH_POLL_SECONDS = 15
LOAD_ALERT_RATIO = 0.9          # 1-minute load over CPU count
LOAD_STRIKES = 3                # consecutive polls over the line before alerting
MEM_AVAIL_MIN_PCT = 15
GPU_TEMP_MAX_C = 85
GPU_QUERY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 5
SECONDS_PER_MINUTE = 60
SECONDS_PER_HOUR = 3600
SECONDS_PER_DAY = 86400


def now_iso():
    return datetime.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds")


def keepalive_seconds():
    margin = min(KEEPALIVE_MARGIN_SECONDS, CACHE_TTL_SECONDS * KEEPALIVE_MARGIN_MAX_FRACTION)
    return max(MIN_KEEPALIVE_SECONDS, CACHE_TTL_SECONDS - margin)


# CARDS R1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the one name check, contract C0.
def valid_name(name):
    """True when `name` may be a mailbox name under C0.

    1 to NAME_MAX_CHARS of letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore, no
    leading dash, not only dots. fullmatch, so a trailing newline fails.

    @interacts  none; string work only.
    @deps       re (NAME_PATTERN).
    @complexity O(c) over c characters in `name`, at most NAME_MAX_CHARS
                before the pattern stops matching.
    @alloc      the stripped copy for the dots check, freed on return.
    """
    return (isinstance(name, str) and NAME_PATTERN.fullmatch(name) is not None
            and name.strip(".") != "")


def require_names(*names):
    """Exit EXIT_BAD_NAME, with C0's words on stderr, at the first bad name.

    None is skipped (an optional name not given). Every command calls this
    before it touches the disk, so a bad name writes nothing.

    @interacts  writes one line to stderr and exits on a bad name; else none.
    @deps       valid_name, sys.
    @complexity O(n * c) over n names given (one to three) of c characters.
    @alloc      the message string on a bad name.
    """
    for name in names:
        if name is not None and not valid_name(name):
            print(f'tc: bad name "{name}": letters, digits, dot, dash, underscore, '
                  f'1 to {NAME_MAX_CHARS}, not starting with a dash', file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(EXIT_BAD_NAME)


# ---------- shared mailbox: one group of Linux users, one mailbox (C4) ----------
# SHARED H1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): .shared, the umask, init, and the
# one helper every folder tc makes goes through (D16).

def shared_group():
    """The group named in ROOT/.shared, or None when the mailbox is private (no .shared).

    Raises ValueError, with words naming the file, when .shared is there
    but read_regular_file will not read it (a symlink, a FIFO, anything
    but a regular file, over SHARED_MARKER_READ_MAX_BYTES, not UTF-8), or
    when it names no group on one line. tc never guesses which group a
    mailbox is shared with: main() refuses every command but init on it
    (set_shared_umask), and an in-process caller sees the ValueError.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared through read_regular_file. Writes
                nothing.
    @deps       read_regular_file; SHARED_MARKER_NAME,
                SHARED_MARKER_READ_MAX_BYTES.
    @complexity O(r) over r bytes read, at most
                SHARED_MARKER_READ_MAX_BYTES + 1.
    @alloc      the file's text and the group string, which is returned.
    """
    path = ROOT / SHARED_MARKER_NAME
    try:
        text = read_regular_file(path, SHARED_MARKER_READ_MAX_BYTES)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except (OSError, ValueError) as err:
        raise ValueError(f"{path} is unreadable ({err})") from err
    group = text.strip()
    if not group or "\n" in group:
        raise ValueError(f"{path} names no group on one line")
    return group


# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-7.
def shared_problem_words(problem):
    """The refusal for a .shared that shared_group would not read (`problem`, its ValueError): what to check, by cause.

    A permission error is not the file's fault: this user cannot search a
    folder above ROOT (a home folder at 0750) or read .shared, and init
    cannot fix that, so the words say what access is needed. Anything else
    (a link, a FIFO, too big, not UTF-8, no group on one line) is the
    file's, and init rewrites it.

    @interacts  none; string work only.
    @deps       ROOT, SHARED_MARKER_NAME.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one string, returned.
    """
    if isinstance(problem.__cause__, PermissionError):
        return (f"tc: cannot read {ROOT / SHARED_MARKER_NAME} ({problem.__cause__.strerror}); this user "
                f"needs search (x) access on every folder from / down to {ROOT}, and read access to "
                f".shared. Nothing done.")
    return f"tc: {problem}; nothing done. Run tc init --shared --group <g> to rewrite it."


def set_shared_umask(fn):
    """Set SHARED_UMASK when ROOT/.shared names a group, before the command `fn` writes anything (C4).

    main() calls it ahead of the command, so every lock it makes is
    group-writable. An unreadable .shared refuses every command (exit 1,
    nothing written, shared_problem_words) except init, which rewrites
    it.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared (shared_group); sets this process's
                umask, or writes one line to stderr and exits.
    @deps       shared_group, shared_problem_words, refuse, cmd_init; os;
                SHARED_UMASK.
    @complexity O(1): one small file.
    @alloc      none past the read.
    """
    try:
        group = shared_group()
    except ValueError as problem:
        if fn is cmd_init:
            return
        refuse(shared_problem_words(problem), EXIT_REFUSED)
    if group is not None:
        os.umask(SHARED_UMASK)


# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20, what a command
# needs to know about sharing, and whose a file is.
# A shared mailbox's group, by name and number. A private mailbox has none.
Share = collections.namedtuple("Share", "group gid")


def mailbox_share():
    """Share(group, gid) when ROOT/.shared names a group, None when the mailbox is private.

    Refuses (exit 1, words) a .shared that is unreadable or names a group
    this machine lacks: tc never guesses which group a mailbox is shared
    with, and nothing is written before the refusal.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared (shared_group) and, when it names a
                group, the group database; on a refusal one line to stderr
                and exit 1.
    @deps       shared_group, shared_problem_words, refuse, printable; grp;
                SHARED_MARKER_NAME, EXIT_REFUSED.
    @complexity O(1): one small file and one group lookup.
    @alloc      one Share, returned.
    """
    try:
        group = shared_group()
    except ValueError as problem:
        refuse(shared_problem_words(problem), EXIT_REFUSED)
    if group is None:
        return None
    try:
        return Share(group, grp.getgrnam(group).gr_gid)
    except KeyError:
        refuse(f"tc: {ROOT / SHARED_MARKER_NAME} names group {printable(group)}, which this "
               f"machine does not have; nothing written", EXIT_REFUSED)


def user_name(uid):
    """The login name of user number `uid`, or the number as text when the machine has no such user.

    @interacts  reads the user database.
    @deps       pwd.
    @complexity O(1): one lookup.
    @alloc      one short string, returned.
    """
    try:
        return pwd.getpwuid(uid).pw_name
    except KeyError:
        return str(uid)


def group_name(gid):
    """The name of group number `gid`, or the number as text when the machine has no such group.

    @interacts  reads the group database.
    @deps       grp.
    @complexity O(1): one lookup.
    @alloc      one short string, returned.
    """
    try:
        return grp.getgrgid(gid).gr_name
    except KeyError:
        return str(gid)


def in_shared_folders(path):
    """True when `path` is mail/, status/, cards/ or outbox/ under ROOT, or anything inside one of them.

    @interacts  none; path work only.
    @deps       pathlib; ROOT, SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES.
    @complexity O(1): four comparisons.
    @alloc      the four paths compared, freed on return.
    """
    return any(path.is_relative_to(ROOT / name) for name in SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES)


def make_folder(path):
    """Make the folder `path`, and any missing folder above it. Every folder tc makes is made here. The mailbox's Share, or None when private.

    A folder already there is left as it is. Under a shared mailbox (D16),
    each folder this call makes gets the group named in .shared and its
    mode from shared_folder_mode (the sticky SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE for
    mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/, D17; SHARED_INBOX_MODE for an
    inbox and its read/, D20) as it is made. Group and mode go through a
    descriptor opened with O_NOFOLLOW, so a link swapped in at the new
    name is refused, never changed. Raises FileExistsError when something
    that is not a folder holds the name, as Path.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    does. Refuses (exit 1) when .shared is unreadable or names a group
    this machine lacks (mailbox_share, before anything is made).

    SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's MEDIUM-4
    and LOW-13. A shared mailbox never ends up with a folder in the wrong
    group. When the new folder cannot be given the group and mode (a
    process outside the group, say), tc removes the empty folder it just
    made, its own runtime cleanup, and refuses with words. Under the four
    shared folders, a folder already there (`path` itself) that is not in
    the group, or is a symlink, is refused with words, never accepted.
    .shared and the group are resolved once per call, not once per level.
    The Share is returned, so a caller that makes its folder first knows
    the mailbox's mode without reading .shared again.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared and the group database (mailbox_share);
                lstats `path` (under the shared folders, shared) or stats
                it, and its missing parents; makes each missing folder;
                under a shared mailbox opens each new folder and sets its
                group and mode, or removes it again; on a refusal one line
                to stderr and exit 1.
    @deps       mailbox_share, make_folder_with, refuse.
    @complexity O(d) over d missing folders: at most three below ROOT
                (mail/, an inbox, its read/), plus ROOT and its missing
                parents on a first run against a new TC_ROOT (private
                only: a shared ROOT holds .shared). O(1) when `path` is
                there. One .shared read and one group lookup either way.
    @alloc      one descriptor per new folder under a shared mailbox,
                closed before return; the Share, returned.
    """
    share = mailbox_share()
    make_folder_with(path, share)
    return share


def make_folder_with(path, share):
    """make_folder's work, with the mailbox's Share (or None) resolved once by the caller and passed down each level.

    @interacts  see make_folder.
    @deps       in_shared_folders, shared_folder_mode, refuse, printable,
                user_name, group_name; os, stat, errno.
    @complexity O(d) over d missing folders, as make_folder.
    @alloc      one descriptor per new folder under a shared mailbox,
                closed before return.
    """
    if share is not None and in_shared_folders(path):
        try:
            st = os.lstat(path)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            st = None
        if st is not None:
            if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
                refuse(f"tc: {path} is a symlink, not a real folder; tc never follows it, so nothing "
                       f"was written there", EXIT_REFUSED)
            if not stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
                raise FileExistsError(errno.EEXIST, "not a folder", str(path))
            if st.st_gid != share.gid:
                refuse(f"tc: {path} is in group {printable(group_name(st.st_gid))}, not "
                       f"{printable(share.group)}; under a shared mailbox every folder under mail/, "
                       f"status/, cards/ and outbox/ is in that group, or other members cannot use it. "
                       f"Nothing written. Its owner, {user_name(st.st_uid)}, can fix it with "
                       f"tc init --shared --group {printable(share.group)}.", EXIT_REFUSED)
            return
    elif path.is_dir():
        return
    if not path.parent.is_dir():
        make_folder_with(path.parent, share)
    try:
        os.mkdir(path)
    except FileExistsError:
        if not path.is_dir():
            raise
        return
    if share is None:
        return
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NOFOLLOW)
        try:
            os.fchown(fd, -1, share.gid)
            os.fchmod(fd, shared_folder_mode(path))  # after fchown, which may clear setgid
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
    except OSError as err:
        try:
            os.rmdir(path)  # tc's own folder, made empty just above: none is left in the wrong group
            undone = "removed it again"
        except OSError as stuck:
            undone = f"could not remove it ({stuck.strerror})"
        refuse(f"tc: could not give the new folder {path} group {printable(share.group)} "
               f"({err.strerror}); {undone}; nothing written. Check that this login is in "
               f"group {printable(share.group)} (id -nG).", EXIT_REFUSED)


def share_entry(path, gid):
    """Give one folder or file that init found group `gid` and its shared mode. (kind, why).

    A folder's mode comes from shared_folder_mode (sticky for the four
    shared folders themselves, D17; SHARED_INBOX_MODE for an inbox and its
    read/, D20); a file's from shared_file_mode (SHARED_WRITABLE_FILE_MODE
    directly in mail/, else SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE, D20).

    kind is "folder" or "file" whenever fstat found one, else None; why
    is None when the entry now has group `gid` and its mode, or the words
    for a skip. Nothing at the name is (None, None). The entry is opened
    with O_NOFOLLOW and O_NONBLOCK and changed through that descriptor, so
    a link is skipped, never followed (D17's rule, in init), and a FIFO
    never blocks. Anything but a folder or a regular file is skipped, and
    so is an entry tc may not change (one another user owns: its owner is
    named).

    SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-2
    and LOW-3. An entry already in group `gid` at its mode is left alone:
    Linux lets only an entry's owner change its group, even to the one it
    has, so a rerun of init on a live mailbox never trips over entries
    other members own. A folder keeps its kind even when it could not be
    changed, so init still walks it.

    @interacts  opens and fstats `path`; sets its group and mode unless
                they are right; reads the user database for a skip's words.
    @deps       shared_folder_mode, shared_file_mode, user_name; os, stat,
                errno.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one descriptor, closed before return.
    """
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None, None
    except OSError as err:
        return None, ("a symlink, which tc never follows" if err.errno == errno.ELOOP else err.strerror)
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
            kind, want = "folder", shared_folder_mode(path)
        elif stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            kind, want = "file", shared_file_mode(path)
        else:
            return None, "not a regular file or a folder"
        if st.st_gid == gid and stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode) == want:
            return kind, None  # SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): already right
        try:
            os.fchown(fd, -1, gid)
            os.fchmod(fd, want)  # after fchown, which may clear setgid
        except OSError as err:
            owner = f" (owned by {user_name(st.st_uid)})" if err.errno == errno.EPERM else ""
            return kind, f"{err.strerror}{owner}"
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    return kind, None


# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the audit's LOW-3 and
# LOW-15, init's walk one entry at a time.
def shared_entries(top, gid):
    """Share `top` and, when it is a folder, every entry under it (share_entry); yield (path, kind, why) for each, one at a time.

    A top folder that could not be changed is still walked, so what
    under it can be changed is, and the rest is counted and named. A
    linked folder is listed, never entered (os.walk's default); a top
    that is a link is not walked at all.

    @interacts  opens, fstats and changes each entry (share_entry); lists
                each folder under `top`.
    @deps       share_entry; os, pathlib.
    @complexity O(f) over f entries under `top` (hundreds to thousands
                under mail/: read/ is the history).
    @alloc      one folder's listing at a time (os.walk); nothing kept.
    """
    kind, why = share_entry(top, gid)
    yield top, kind, why
    if kind != "folder":
        return
    for here, folders, files in os.walk(top):
        for entry in folders + files:
            path = pathlib.Path(here) / entry
            yield (path, *share_entry(path, gid))


# SHARED D17 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the sticky folders, opens that never
# follow a link, and the inbox folder checked before use.
def shared_folder_mode(path):
    """The mode a shared folder gets: SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE for the four, SHARED_INBOX_MODE for an inbox and its read/, else SHARED_FOLDER_MODE.

    The sticky bit goes on mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ themselves
    (D17). SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): an inbox
    (mail/to-<name>) and its read/ take SHARED_INBOX_MODE, sticky with no
    access for other users (D20 item 1). Under sticky the recipient, who
    owns the inbox, still moves every message into read/, and no sender
    can touch another's.

    @interacts  none; path work only.
    @deps       pathlib; ROOT, MAIL, SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES,
                SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE, SHARED_INBOX_MODE,
                SHARED_FOLDER_MODE.
    @complexity O(1): a few name comparisons.
    @alloc      the parent paths it compares, freed on return.
    """
    if path.parent == ROOT and path.name in SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES:
        return SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE
    box = path.parent if path.name == "read" else path
    if box.parent == MAIL and box.name.startswith("to-"):
        return SHARED_INBOX_MODE
    return SHARED_FOLDER_MODE


def shared_file_mode(path):
    """The mode init gives a file under the shared folders: SHARED_WRITABLE_FILE_MODE directly in mail/, else SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE.

    SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20 items 2 and 5.
    The files directly in mail/ are the ones every member must write
    (mail/.seq, the locks, the ring marker); messages live one level
    down, in an inbox or its read/. Cards, status files and messages are
    never group-writable.

    @interacts  none; path work only.
    @deps       pathlib; MAIL, SHARED_WRITABLE_FILE_MODE,
                SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      none.
    """
    return SHARED_WRITABLE_FILE_MODE if path.parent == MAIL else SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE


# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D19, a FIFO at a lock's
# name is refused at once (O_NONBLOCK, then S_ISREG).
def open_regular(path, flags, mode=NEW_FILE_MODE):
    """Open the regular file at `path` with `flags`, making it at `mode` when nothing is there. A descriptor.

    Every open adds O_NOFOLLOW and O_NONBLOCK (D17, D19), and fstat checks
    the result before it is returned. So a symlink raises OSError (errno
    ELOOP), a FIFO never blocks (OSError ENXIO when it has no reader,
    else ValueError), a folder raises OSError (EISDIR) or ValueError, and
    anything else that is not a regular file raises ValueError. The
    descriptor returned is blocking again. A file already there is opened
    without O_CREAT, and only a missing one is made, with O_CREAT and
    O_EXCL (`mode` before the umask), then opened again if another
    process made it in between. The split matters under C4: in a sticky,
    group-writable folder (mail/, status/), Linux with
    fs.protected_regular = 2 refuses an O_CREAT open of a file that
    another user made, even one the group may write.

    @interacts  opens and fstats one file, creating it empty when missing.
    @deps       os, stat; NEW_FILE_MODE.
    @complexity O(1): at most three open calls and one fstat.
    @alloc      one descriptor, returned (the caller closes it), or closed
                before anything is raised.
    """
    problem = None
    for how in (flags, flags | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, flags):
        try:
            fd = os.open(path, how | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK, mode)
        except (FileNotFoundError, FileExistsError) as err:
            problem = err  # missing: make it; made in between: open that one
            continue
        try:
            if not stat.S_ISREG(os.fstat(fd).st_mode):
                raise ValueError("it is not a regular file")
            os.set_blocking(fd, True)
        except BaseException:
            os.close(fd)
            raise
        return fd
    raise problem


def open_by_name(path, flags, mode=NEW_FILE_MODE):
    """open_regular, refusing (exit 1, words) a symlink, a FIFO or anything else that is not a regular file. A descriptor.

    For each file tc opens by a name a group member can also write (D17,
    D19): the wait, watch and inbox locks, mail/.seq and
    status/<name>.md. A symlink at the name, dangling or not, is refused,
    and so is a FIFO, at once instead of hanging tc; nothing is read or
    written through either. SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5,
    unreviewed): a file this user may not open (another member's status
    file, 0640 under D20) is refused with words naming its owner, never a
    traceback.

    @interacts  see open_regular; on a refusal, lstats the name for its
                owner, reads the user database, writes one line to stderr
                and exits 1.
    @deps       open_regular, refuse, user_name; errno, os; NEW_FILE_MODE,
                EXIT_REFUSED.
    @complexity O(1): at most three open calls and one fstat.
    @alloc      one descriptor, returned; the caller closes it.
    """
    try:
        return open_regular(path, flags, mode)
    except ValueError:
        problem = "is not a regular file, which tc never opens"
    except OSError as err:
        if err.errno == errno.ELOOP:
            problem = "is a symlink, which tc never follows"
        elif err.errno in (errno.ENXIO, errno.EISDIR):  # a FIFO with no reader, a folder
            problem = "is not a regular file, which tc never opens"
        elif err.errno == errno.EACCES:  # SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20
            try:
                owner = user_name(os.lstat(path).st_uid)
            except OSError:
                owner = "another user"
            problem = (f"belongs to {owner}, and this user may not open it ({err.strerror}); under a "
                       f"shared mailbox only its owner writes it")
        else:
            raise
    refuse(f"tc: {path} {problem}; nothing written through it", EXIT_REFUSED)


def check_inbox(name, shared):
    """Refuse (exit 1) a bad mail/to-<name> or read/: a link or not a folder (D17), or, when `shared`, not its card owner's (D20). (box, read/) lstat results.

    Checked with lstat before tc uses an inbox (send, read, peek, wait),
    so a symlink planted at an inbox's name, or at its read/, is refused
    and no mail is written, moved or read through it. Nothing at the name
    passes: each result is None for what is not there.

    SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20 item 4. Under a
    shared mailbox an inbox, or its read/, whose owner differs from the
    owner of cards/<name>.json is refused with words: an old inbox, or one
    a sender made before D20, sits in a folder its recipient does not
    control, and tc never guesses which owner is right. With no card
    there, nothing is compared.

    @interacts  lstats mail/to-<name>, its read/ and, when `shared` and the
                inbox is there, cards/<name>.json; reads the user database
                for the words; on a bad one, one line to stderr and exit 1.
    @deps       refuse, user_name; os, stat; MAIL, CARDS, EXIT_REFUSED.
    @complexity O(1): at most three lstat calls.
    @alloc      up to three stat results; the two for the inbox returned.
    """
    box = MAIL / f"to-{name}"
    found = []
    for path in (box, box / "read"):
        try:
            st = os.lstat(path)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            break
        if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
            refuse(f"tc: {path} is a symlink, not a real folder; tc never follows it, so nothing "
                   f"was read or written there", EXIT_REFUSED)
        if not stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
            refuse(f"tc: {path} is not a folder; nothing was read or written there", EXIT_REFUSED)
        found.append(st)
    box_st, read_st = (found + [None, None])[:2]
    if shared and box_st is not None:
        card = CARDS / f"{name}.json"
        try:
            card_uid = os.lstat(card).st_uid
        except FileNotFoundError:
            card_uid = None
        for path, st in ((box, box_st), (box / "read", read_st)):
            if card_uid is not None and st is not None and st.st_uid != card_uid:
                refuse(f"tc: {path} belongs to {user_name(st.st_uid)}, but its card {card} belongs to "
                       f"{user_name(card_uid)}; under a shared mailbox an inbox belongs to the owner of "
                       f"its card, and tc never guesses which one is right. Nothing was read or written "
                       f"there.", EXIT_REFUSED)
    return box_st, read_st


# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20 item 1, tc join makes the inbox.
def claim_inbox(name):
    """Under a shared mailbox, make mail/to-<name>/ and its read/ as this user, the recipient (D20 item 1); refuse a name another user holds.

    tc join calls it before it writes anything. Refused (exit 1, words,
    nothing made or written): a card at cards/<name>.json that another
    user owns (under the sticky cards/ only its owner can replace it), or
    an inbox or read/ there that another user owns. An inbox this user
    already owns is kept as it is, and a missing read/ is made. A folder
    made here gets group g and SHARED_INBOX_MODE (make_folder).

    @interacts  lstats cards/<name>.json, mail/to-<name> and its read/
                (check_inbox); reads this process's user id and the user
                database; makes the missing folders (make_folder); on a
                refusal one line to stderr and exit 1.
    @deps       check_inbox, make_folder, refuse, user_name; os; MAIL,
                CARDS, EXIT_REFUSED.
    @complexity O(1): three lstat calls and at most two new folders.
    @alloc      the stat results, freed on return.
    """
    me = os.geteuid()
    card = CARDS / f"{name}.json"
    try:
        owner = os.lstat(card).st_uid
    except FileNotFoundError:
        owner = me
    if owner != me:
        refuse(f"tc join: {card} belongs to {user_name(owner)}; under a shared mailbox only its owner "
               f"can replace it. No card written; pick another name, or ask {user_name(owner)}.",
               EXIT_REFUSED)
    box = MAIL / f"to-{name}"
    box_st, read_st = check_inbox(name, False)
    for path, st in ((box, box_st), (box / "read", read_st)):
        if st is not None and st.st_uid != me:
            refuse(f"tc join: {path} belongs to {user_name(st.st_uid)}; under a shared mailbox an inbox "
                   f"belongs to its recipient, so {name} is taken here. No card written; pick another "
                   f"name, or ask {user_name(st.st_uid)}.", EXIT_REFUSED)
    if read_st is None:
        make_folder(box / "read")


# SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): .shared belongs to the user who ran init.
def init_user_words(marker):
    """D21's words for a .shared at `marker` that another user owns: `; only the user who ran init (<owner>) can change the group`, else "".

    "" when this user owns it, and when nothing at `marker` can be
    lstat()ed (gone, or out of reach), since there is no owner to name.

    @interacts  lstats `marker`; reads the user database when another
                user owns it.
    @deps       user_name; os.
    @complexity O(1): one lstat and at most one user lookup.
    @alloc      one string, returned.
    """
    try:
        owner = os.lstat(marker).st_uid
    except OSError:
        return ""
    if owner == os.geteuid():
        return ""
    return f"; only the user who ran init ({user_name(owner)}) can change the group"


def settle_marker_mode(marker):
    """Set the .shared at `marker` to SHARED_MARKER_MODE when this user owns it at another mode (D21); leave anyone else's alone.

    For init's rerun with the group .shared already names, so a .shared
    written before D21 (0664) is made owner-write only by its owner. It
    is opened with O_NOFOLLOW and O_NONBLOCK and changed through that
    descriptor, never through a link. Another user's .shared, or anything
    that is not a regular file, is left as it is. A change that fails is
    refused (exit 1, words); the rest of init is done by then.

    @interacts  opens and fstats `marker` and, when it is this user's at
                another mode, fchmods it; on a failure one line to stderr
                and exit 1.
    @deps       refuse; os, stat; SHARED_MARKER_MODE, EXIT_REFUSED.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one descriptor, closed before return.
    """
    try:
        fd = os.open(marker, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK)
        try:
            st = os.fstat(fd)
            if (stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) and st.st_uid == os.geteuid()
                    and stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode) != SHARED_MARKER_MODE):
                os.fchmod(fd, SHARED_MARKER_MODE)
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
    except OSError as err:
        refuse(f"tc init: could not set {marker} to mode {SHARED_MARKER_MODE:04o} ({err.strerror}); "
               f"run tc init again, or set it by hand", EXIT_REFUSED)


# SHARED H1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): tc init --shared (C4, D19, D20).
def cmd_init(args):
    """`tc init --shared --group <g>`: share this mailbox with group g (C4).

    Refuses (exit 1, nothing changed) a group this machine lacks or one
    the running user is not in: os.getgroups(), plus the effective group,
    which on some systems getgroups() leaves out. Then sets group g and
    SHARED_TOP_FOLDER_MODE (sticky, D17) on mail/, status/, cards/ and
    outbox/ (those that exist), and on every folder and file already under
    them group g and the mode for its kind (share_entry): SHARED_INBOX_MODE
    for an inbox and its read/, SHARED_FOLDER_MODE for any other folder,
    SHARED_WRITABLE_FILE_MODE for mail/.seq, the locks and the ring marker,
    SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE for every message, card and status file (D20).
    It cannot change who owns an entry. Folders made later get the same
    from make_folder (D16).
    Last, it writes ROOT/.shared holding g, through a .tmp and a rename.
    An entry it cannot change (a link, a FIFO, one another user owns) is
    named on stderr, and init exits 1 once the rest is done.

    SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): .shared is made
    SHARED_MARKER_MODE (0644), so only the user who ran init can rewrite
    it. tc never makes it group-writable. A rerun that finds it naming g
    does not rewrite it, and when this user owns it at another mode (0664
    from before D21) sets it to SHARED_MARKER_MODE (settle_marker_mode).
    When .shared names another group and another user owns it, init
    refuses first, before it makes, walks or changes anything: exit 1,
    the mailbox stays shared with that group, and only the user who ran
    init, named, can change the group (init_user_words). The same words
    end the refusal when init may not replace a .shared another user
    owns for any other reason (an unreadable one).

    SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D19 and the audit's
    LOW-2 to LOW-4 and LOW-7. Init first makes ROOT (it is not shared:
    no group, no mode) and then mail/, status/, cards/ and outbox/ when
    they are missing, so no other user ever has to make a top folder in a
    ROOT the first user owns. ROOT it cannot reach is refused with words
    on search access, nothing changed. Entries already right are left
    alone (share_entry), so a rerun on a live mailbox is clean, and a top
    folder whose own change failed is still walked (shared_entries). The
    skipped lines print before anything else is said. When .shared
    already names g it is not rewritten; when it cannot be written, the
    words say the mailbox's real state: still private, still shared with
    the group .shared names, or .shared still unreadable.

    @interacts  reads the group database and this process's groups;
                reads ROOT/.shared (shared_group) and, when it names
                another group, lstats it for its owner (init_user_words);
                sets its umask (SHARED_UMASK); makes ROOT and its missing
                parents, and each missing top folder; walks the four
                folders and opens, fstats and changes each entry
                (shared_entries); when .shared names g, opens it and sets
                its mode if this user owns it (settle_marker_mode); else
                creates ROOT/.shared.<pid>.tmp at SHARED_MARKER_MODE and
                renames it to ROOT/.shared, removing its own .tmp if the
                rename fails, and on a permission error lstats
                ROOT/.shared for its owner and reads the user database.
                Prints one line, plus one stderr line per skipped entry,
                first.
    @deps       shared_entries, shared_group, init_user_words,
                settle_marker_mode, write_new_file, refuse, printable; grp,
                os, sys; SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES, SHARED_MARKER_NAME,
                SHARED_MARKER_MODE, SHARED_UMASK.
    @complexity O(f) over f folders and files under the four folders: the
                inboxes and every read/ (hundreds to thousands, growing
                with the history).
    @alloc      one folder's listing at a time (os.walk) and one skipped
                line per entry it could not change, freed on exit; the
                four top folders when missing and the .shared file, kept.
    """
    group = args.group
    try:
        gid = grp.getgrnam(group).gr_gid
    except KeyError:
        refuse(f"tc init: no group named {printable(group)} on this machine; nothing changed", EXIT_REFUSED)
    if gid not in os.getgroups() and gid != os.getegid():
        refuse(f"tc init: you are not in group {printable(group)}; nothing changed", EXIT_REFUSED)
    marker = ROOT / SHARED_MARKER_NAME
    try:
        current = shared_group()
    except ValueError:
        current = ValueError  # unreadable: init is the one command that runs past it
    # SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): another member's init that would change the group changes nothing.
    only = init_user_words(marker) if isinstance(current, str) and current != group else ""
    if only:
        refuse(f"tc init: may not change {marker}; the mailbox stays shared with group "
               f"{printable(current)}{only}. Nothing changed.", EXIT_REFUSED)
    os.umask(SHARED_UMASK)
    try:
        os.makedirs(ROOT, exist_ok=True)  # ROOT itself is not shared: no group, no mode
    except OSError as err:
        refuse(f"tc init: cannot reach {ROOT} ({err.strerror}); this user needs search (x) access on "
               f"every folder from / down to it. Nothing changed.", EXIT_REFUSED)
    done, skipped = {"folder": 0, "file": 0}, []
    for top in (ROOT / name for name in SHARED_FOLDER_NAMES):
        try:
            os.mkdir(top)  # D19: no other user has to make a top folder in a ROOT this user owns
        except FileExistsError:
            pass
        except OSError as err:
            skipped.append(f"tc init: could not make {printable(top)}: {err.strerror}")
            continue
        for path, kind, why in shared_entries(top, gid):
            if why is not None:
                skipped.append(f"tc init: skipped {printable(path)}: {why}")
            elif kind is not None:
                done[kind] += 1
    for line in skipped:  # reported first, whatever happens to .shared
        print(line, file=sys.stderr)
    if current == group:
        settle_marker_mode(marker)  # SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): its owner's rerun makes it 0644
    else:
        tmp, made = ROOT / f"{SHARED_MARKER_NAME}.{os.getpid()}.tmp", False
        try:
            write_new_file(tmp, f"{group}\n", SHARED_MARKER_MODE)  # SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
            made = True
            tmp.rename(marker)  # atomic, so a reader never sees half of it
        except OSError as err:
            if made:
                tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)  # init's own .tmp, made just above
            state = ("the mailbox stays private" if current is None
                     else f"{marker} stays unreadable, so tc refuses every command but init"
                     if current is ValueError else f"the mailbox stays shared with group {printable(current)}")
            if current is not None and isinstance(err, PermissionError):
                state += init_user_words(marker)  # SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
            refuse(f"tc init: could not write {marker} ({err.strerror}); {state}. {done['folder']} "
                   f"folders and {done['file']} files are in group {printable(group)}.", EXIT_REFUSED)
    print(f"mailbox shared with group {printable(group)}: {done['folder']} folders and "
          f"{done['file']} files in group {printable(group)}")
    if skipped:
        refuse(f"tc init: {len(skipped)} skipped; run tc init again as their owner, or fix them by hand",
               EXIT_REFUSED)


def inbox(name, shared):
    """mail/to-<name>/, checked; in a private mailbox made with its read/ when missing (through make_folder, D16).

    Refuses (exit 1) an inbox or read/ that is a symlink or not a folder
    (check_inbox, D17), so every send, read, peek and wait poll is checked.
    SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): when `shared`, an
    inbox whose owner is not its card's owner is refused too, and nothing
    is made: only tc join makes an inbox there, as its recipient (D20).
    The path is returned whether or not the folder is there.

    @interacts  lstats mail/to-<name>, its read/ and, when `shared`, the
                card (check_inbox); private only: makes mail/,
                mail/to-<name>/ and its read/ when read/ is missing.
    @deps       check_inbox, make_folder.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      the path, returned.
    """
    box = MAIL / f"to-{name}"
    box_st, read_st = check_inbox(name, shared)  # SHARED D17 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
    if not shared and read_st is None:
        make_folder(box / "read")  # SHARED H1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D16
    return box


# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D19, a message is a
# regular file; anything else among them is skipped and named, never read.
def real_folder(path):
    """True when lstat finds a real folder at `path`: not a symlink to one, not missing.

    @interacts  lstats `path`. Writes nothing.
    @deps       os, stat.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one stat result, freed on return.
    """
    try:
        return stat.S_ISDIR(os.lstat(path).st_mode)
    except OSError:
        return False


def message_files(folder):
    """The *.md entries in `folder` as (messages, skipped): (path, lstat result) for each regular file, by name; (path, why) for the rest (D19).

    Checked with lstat, never followed: a symlink, a FIFO or a folder
    named like a message is skipped, so read, peek, log, wait and the
    counts leave it out, and no wait wakes for it. A missing folder holds
    nothing, and an entry gone before its lstat is left out. The caller
    makes sure `folder` itself is a real folder.

    @interacts  lists `folder` and lstats each *.md entry. Writes nothing.
    @deps       os, stat, pathlib.
    @complexity O(e) over e *.md entries: single digits in an inbox; a
                read/ is the history (hundreds to thousands), listed only
                by log and health.
    @alloc      two lists, returned.
    """
    messages, skipped = [], []
    for path in sorted(folder.glob("*.md")):
        try:
            st = os.lstat(path)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue
        if stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            messages.append((path, st))
        else:
            skipped.append((path, "it is a symlink, which tc never follows" if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode)
                            else "it is not a regular file"))
    return messages, skipped


def inbox_messages(name, shared=None):
    """The unread mail in mail/to-<name>/ as (paths, skipped): the regular files, by name, and (path, why) for each other *.md entry.

    Goes through inbox(), so a linked inbox or read/ refuses (exit 1,
    check_inbox), and in a private mailbox a missing one is made; in a
    shared one a missing inbox holds nothing. `shared` None means look it
    up here (mailbox_share); a caller that polls passes what it found once.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared when `shared` is None; see inbox and
                message_files.
    @deps       mailbox_share, inbox, message_files.
    @complexity O(u) over u *.md entries in the inbox (single digits).
    @alloc      two lists, returned.
    """
    if shared is None:
        shared = mailbox_share() is not None
    messages, skipped = message_files(inbox(name, shared))
    return [path for path, _ in messages], skipped


def unread(name, shared=None):
    """The unread messages in mail/to-<name>/: each *.md there that is a regular file, by name (D19).

    `shared` as in inbox_messages.

    @interacts  see inbox_messages.
    @deps       inbox_messages.
    @complexity O(u) over u *.md entries in the inbox (single digits).
    @alloc      one list of paths, returned.
    """
    return inbox_messages(name, shared)[0]


def next_seq():
    """The next message number: mail/.seq read, raised by one and written back under its flock.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared and makes mail/ when missing
                (make_folder); opens mail/.seq through open_by_name,
                creating it when missing and refusing a symlink or a FIFO
                there (exit 1, D17, D19), and holds its flock while it
                reads and rewrites the number.
    @deps       make_folder, open_by_name; fcntl, os.
    @complexity O(1).
    @alloc      one open file, closed on return.
    """
    make_folder(MAIL)  # SHARED H1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D16
    with open(open_by_name(SEQ_FILE, os.O_RDWR | os.O_APPEND), "a+") as f:  # SHARED D17 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
        fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        f.seek(0)
        n = int(f.read().strip() or 0) + 1
        f.seek(0)
        f.truncate()
        f.write(str(n))
    return n


# CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the inbox lock (D14), for send, read and card.
@contextlib.contextmanager
def inbox_lock(name, how=fcntl.LOCK_EX):
    """Hold mail/.box-<name>.lock (flock) for the `with` block.

    One lock per inbox. Held alone (LOCK_EX) by the codex route from its
    unread count to its ring marker, by `tc read` from its glob to its
    last rename, by `tc card` from its unread count to its card's rename,
    and by `tc join` for a codex card from its card's rename to its ring
    marker (D16). A local send holds it shared (LOCK_SH) from its card read
    to its rename: local senders never queue behind each other, only
    behind those four. So a send never lands between a reader's glob and
    its renames (the lost wake, D14), and never lands in an inbox after a
    `tc card` for that name counted it, where it would never be read once
    the card says ssh. Nothing slow runs under it: the codex ring waits
    until it is released, and `tc read` prints after it is released, so a
    reader stalled on its stdout never blocks a send.

    @interacts  reads ROOT/.shared and makes mail/ (make_folder, D16);
                opens mail/.box-<name>.lock through open_by_name, creating
                it empty (append mode, so it never truncates what is there)
                and refusing a symlink or a FIFO there (exit 1, D17, D19),
                and holds the flock until the block ends; the kernel drops
                it if the process dies.
    @deps       make_folder, open_by_name; contextlib, fcntl, os.
    @complexity O(1), plus however long another holder keeps it.
    @alloc      one open file for the block; mail/.box-<name>.lock, made on
                first use and kept (nothing removes it).
    """
    make_folder(MAIL)  # SHARED H1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D16
    with open(open_by_name(MAIL / f".box-{name}.lock", os.O_WRONLY | os.O_APPEND), "a") as lock:  # SHARED D17 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
        fcntl.flock(lock, how)
        yield


# CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): a link planted at a .tmp name is refused.
def write_new_file(path, text, mode=NEW_FILE_MODE):
    """Create `path`, which must not exist yet, at `mode`, and write `text` into it.

    For every .tmp tc renames into place (a message, a card, .shared).
    NEW_FILE_FLAGS fail on anything already at that name and never follow
    a link, so a link planted at a .tmp name is refused, not written
    through. Raises OSError (FileExistsError for a planted link or file);
    the caller refuses with words. The umask applies to `mode`, and the
    text is encoded as Path.write_text would. SHARED fix round 1 (Claude
    Opus 5.5, unreviewed): a message or card under a shared mailbox passes
    SHARED_OWNED_FILE_MODE (D20 item 2); the umask cannot add bits to it.
    SHARED D21 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): .shared passes
    SHARED_MARKER_MODE.

    @interacts  creates and writes one file; nothing else.
    @deps       os; NEW_FILE_FLAGS, NEW_FILE_MODE.
    @complexity O(t) over t characters of `text`.
    @alloc      the encoded text while written; one descriptor, closed on
                return.
    """
    with open(os.open(path, NEW_FILE_FLAGS, mode), "w") as f:
        f.write(text)


def cmd_send(args):
    """`tc send`: check, then hand the message to the route on the recipient's card (C2).

    No card means route local: today's send, with today's stdout line. A
    local delivery reads the card again under the inbox lock (shared) and
    lands under it, so a `tc card` for this name either counts this message
    or is read here first. The codex route takes the lock itself.
    The route's Delivery decides the rest: words to stdout on exit 0, else
    to stderr; every warning to stderr; then its exit code.

    @interacts  checks both names first (C0, exit 6, nothing written); reads
                the body (stdin when no text is given) and refuses one over
                SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES (exit 8) or empty (exit 1) before any
                number is taken; reads cards/<to>.json and refuses (exit 1,
                nothing written) a card it cannot route; for a local
                delivery holds mail/.box-<to>.lock shared and reads the card
                again; calls one ROUTES function, which writes the message;
                prints.
    @deps       require_names, read_body, refuse, card_route, inbox_lock,
                deliver_local, Delivery; fcntl.
    @complexity O(b) over b bytes of body, at most SEND_BODY_MAX_BYTES,
                plus the route's own work.
    @alloc      the body string, freed on exit; whatever the route keeps.
    """
    require_names(args.sender, args.to)  # CARDS R1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
    # CARDS R3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): size, then the card, then the route.
    body = read_body(args.message)
    if not body.strip():
        refuse("tc send: empty message", EXIT_REFUSED)  # CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
    card, deliver = card_route(args.to)
    if deliver is deliver_local:
        # CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): the card read again under the shared lock.
        with inbox_lock(args.to, fcntl.LOCK_SH):
            card, deliver = card_route(args.to)
            if deliver is deliver_local:
                result = deliver_local(card, args.sender, args.to, body)
    if deliver is not deliver_local:
        result = deliver(card, args.sender, args.to, body)
    print(result.words, file=sys.stdout if result.exit == 0 else sys.stderr)
    for warning in result.warnings:
        print(warning, file=sys.stderr)
    if result.exit != 0:
        sys.exit(result.exit)


# SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D19, every message
# printed is read through read_regular_file.
def show(paths):
    """Print each message at `paths`, then `---`; return (path, why) for each one read_regular_file will not read (D19).

    Each file is opened with O_NOFOLLOW and O_NONBLOCK and read only when
    fstat calls it a regular file of at most MESSAGE_READ_MAX_BYTES, so a
    link put in place of a message is never followed and a FIFO never
    blocks. Line ends print as read_text gave them (universal newlines).

    @interacts  opens, fstats and reads each file; prints to stdout.
    @deps       read_regular_file; MESSAGE_READ_MAX_BYTES.
    @complexity O(u + b) over u paths and b bytes read, each file at most
                MESSAGE_READ_MAX_BYTES + 1.
    @alloc      each file's text while it prints; the list of the unshown,
                returned.
    """
    unshown = []
    for p in paths:
        try:
            text = read_regular_file(p, MESSAGE_READ_MAX_BYTES)
        except (OSError, ValueError) as err:
            unshown.append((p, err.strerror if isinstance(err, OSError) and err.strerror else str(err)))
            continue
        print(text.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n"))
        print("---")
    return unshown


def refuse_lines(lines):
    """When `lines` holds any: print them to stderr, one each, and exit 1. Else nothing.

    @interacts  writes to stderr and exits when `lines` is not empty.
    @deps       refuse; EXIT_REFUSED.
    @complexity O(n) over n lines (single digits).
    @alloc      the joined text.
    """
    if lines:
        refuse("\n".join(lines), EXIT_REFUSED)


def cmd_read(args):
    """`tc read <name>`: print unread mail and move it to read/.

    The lock covers the glob and the renames only. The mail is printed
    from read/ after the lock is released, so a reader whose stdout stalls
    (a pager left open, a Ctrl-Z) never blocks a send to this name. The
    price: a print that fails after the renames leaves that mail in read/
    unseen, where `tc log` still shows it.

    Only regular files move (D19). A symlink, a FIFO or a folder named
    like a message is left in place, named on stderr and never read; so is
    a moved file read_regular_file will not read. Either makes the read
    exit 1 after it prints the rest. SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5,
    unreviewed): so does a message the rename may not move. Under a shared
    mailbox (D20) only the inbox's owner moves another member's mail, so a
    read run by anyone else moves only that user's own messages and names
    the rest, left unread, never a traceback.

    @interacts  checks the name first (C0, exit 6, nothing written); a name
                with no inbox gets `no unread mail` and nothing is created
                (O2); else reads ROOT/.shared (mailbox_share), holds
                mail/.box-<name>.lock alone from its glob to its last rename
                (D14), refuses an inbox or read/ that is a symlink, or under
                a shared mailbox not its card owner's (exit 1, nothing
                moved: check_inbox, D17, D20), in a private mailbox makes
                mail/to-<name>/read/ if missing, lstats each *.md entry and
                renames each regular file into read/; then, with the lock
                released, reads each moved file (read_regular_file) and
                prints to stdout, and names each one it will not show or
                could not move on stderr.
    @deps       require_names, mailbox_share, inbox_lock, inbox_messages,
                show, refuse_lines, printable.
    @complexity O(u) over u *.md entries in the inbox (single digits).
    @alloc      the lists of paths and each file's text while printed; one
                open lock file while the lock is held.
    """
    require_names(args.name)  # CARDS R1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed)
    if not (MAIL / f"to-{args.name}").is_dir():  # CARDS R3 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): O2
        print(f"no unread mail for {args.name}")
        return
    # CARDS fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D14. A codex send
    # cannot land between this glob and these renames, so it never skips its
    # ring for mail this read did not print.
    # Phase fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): print after the
    # release, so a stalled stdout holds no lock.
    shared = mailbox_share() is not None  # SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20
    with inbox_lock(args.name):
        messages, skipped = inbox_messages(args.name, shared)  # SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D19
        moved, stuck = [], []
        for p in messages:
            try:
                p.rename(p.parent / "read" / p.name)
            except OSError as err:  # SHARED fix round 1 (Claude Opus 5.5, unreviewed): D20, not this user's to move
                stuck.append((p, err.strerror))
                continue
            moved.append(p.parent / "read" / p.name)
    if not moved and not stuck:  # mail it could not move is still unread
        print(f"no unread mail for {args.name}")
    unshown = show(moved)
    refuse_lines([f"tc read: {printable(p)} not shown and left in place ({why})" for p, why in skipped]
                 + [f"tc read: {printable(p)} not moved to read/ ({why}); left unread" for p, why in stuck]
                 + [f"tc read: {printable(p)} not shown ({why})" for p, why in unshown])


